WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.680
I am a hipster, I am an honest-to-god,&nbsp;
dyed-in-the-wool EDH hipster. I like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:11.680
play weird, unusual decks that do weird, unusual&nbsp;
things with weird, unusual cards. The idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.160
I might be playing a deck that does something&nbsp;
nobody has done before is extremely cool to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:21.760
and that specific task is something that is far&nbsp;
more possible to do in EDH than in just about any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:26.400
other format. The format has a card selection&nbsp;
that’s almost as wide as Vintage’s, it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.000
thousands of different options for commanders, and&nbsp;
the fact that it doesn’t have a single set power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.920
level means that no deck is fully squeezed out&nbsp;
as long as you’re willing to shop around for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.720
appropriate table to play at. The level of freedom&nbsp;
that the format offers to deck builders can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:45.440
genuinely overwhelming for the people who aren’t&nbsp;
used to that, but for the most dedicated brewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.760
of the world it’s basically one big playground of&nbsp;
possibilities. Do you have a funky vision you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:57.000
to realize? EDH can absolutely accommodate that. I&nbsp;
do firmly believe all that about the format, and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.720
have long had the goal of pushing the envelope&nbsp;
on how weird and niche casual EDH can get,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.960
but for the past couple months I’ve also had a&nbsp;
nagging thought. Is the version of the EDH format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:14.200
I’m describing actually a good one? One that leads&nbsp;
the most people to have the most fun? Is having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:19.520
weird-off with the table where everybody tries to&nbsp;
do the most performatively unusual things actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.400
a good way to play Magic? Might it even be more&nbsp;
ethical, better to one’s fellow Magic players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.880
to play a deck that’s simpler or does&nbsp;
something more common? So in this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:34.320
I’m going to explore that nagging doubt, but&nbsp;
first I need to lay out a bit of context.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.680
The period between 2002 and 2011 saw the EDH&nbsp;
format grow from a scarcely-known novelty to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.000
a format Wizards of the Coast was willing&nbsp;
to print cards specifically for. A big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.600
step in this process was its adoption as a&nbsp;
casual pickup format among Pro Tour judges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.920
with the late Sheldon Menery effectively&nbsp;
serving as the format’s guide and foremost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.280
advocate during this period and beyond. The&nbsp;
appeal here is clear—it was a more casual way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:02.960
of playing that could manifest at events once&nbsp;
the more cutthroat 1v1 matches of the day had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.600
been concluded. Even after the first precons were&nbsp;
released, this culture of pickup games remained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:13.480
strong across the format. Anecdotally, I played&nbsp;
a decent amount of commander during downtime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:18.680
at local limited and standard events in 2014 and&nbsp;
2015, and it just made sense as a format to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:23.520
when people were looking for a bit of variety.&nbsp;
In his article for Polygon a few years back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.440
Charlie Hall highlighted some of the appeals—if&nbsp;
a handful of people want to play a game of Magic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.760
rather than figuring out an appropriate format to&nbsp;
play or double checking that a couple casual decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:38.000
are built under similar ethos, commander offers an&nbsp;
elegant, one-size-fits-all solution with a broad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.560
card pool and a slim banlist. Though EDH has&nbsp;
since become its own main event in most cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.160
played among friend groups and in dedicated&nbsp;
leagues at local game stores, there are visible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.320
indications of the lingering influence of pickup&nbsp;
games in the direction Wizards of the Coast has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.320
taken with the products they market to commander&nbsp;
players. While they used to sell a product called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:01.440
a “Deckbuilder’s Toolkit” as a way for casual&nbsp;
players to build their first deck on their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.760
time, these got phased out a few years ago to&nbsp;
be replaced with greater Arena support and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:13.080
products for commander, the now-de-facto casual&nbsp;
format of paper magic. Relevant to my point here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.280
most of these products are preconstructed&nbsp;
commander decks, which are ready-to-play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:21.040
out of the box—if you’re sitting in a game store&nbsp;
and a few other people start to play commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:24.560
you can walk up to the counter to buy a precon&nbsp;
and some sleeves and you’ll be good to go in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:28.720
a few minutes, assuming you have a couple&nbsp;
helpful compatriots to help you sleeve up.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:33.560
Now, at this point, you may be saying “but Alex,&nbsp;
how is this relevant?” Well, let’s take a minute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:38.160
to think about the pickup EDH experience, where&nbsp;
you sit down at a table and start up a casual EDH&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.920
game with three unfamiliar opponents who are&nbsp;
playing largely unfamiliar decks. Put simply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.120
it can be a little overwhelming sometimes,&nbsp;
and I’m speaking purely from an information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:52.080
standpoint. With the format being slow and four&nbsp;
player, the board gets gradually clogged with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.600
increasing numbers of assorted permanents of&nbsp;
various stripes, with the purpose of any single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:01.600
one being potentially unclear. And this is EDH&nbsp;
in the year of our lord 2024, so all of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:06.520
have a lot of text, too much text some might&nbsp;
argue. With thousands of commander options,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:09.160
there’s a good chance of at least one of&nbsp;
these at the table being one you haven’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:13.960
played against. Knowledge of how your opponents&nbsp;
plan to win will likely be incomplete at best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:18.360
and next-to-nonexistent at worst if nobody&nbsp;
bothered to pose that question to the table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:22.720
Pickup EDH can be an absolute information&nbsp;
overload, and this broader problem can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:28.000
broken down into two separate sub-problems:&nbsp;
card diversity, and gameplay diversity.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.920
As far as card diversity goes, the EDH format&nbsp;
has tens of thousands of cards available to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:37.840
and a much less restrictive banlist than Legacy&nbsp;
which otherwise shares a similar card pool. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.960
only this, but since the format is 100-card&nbsp;
singleton, seeing any singular card in a deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.600
gives far fewer implications for what the rest of&nbsp;
the deck might contain compared to other formats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:52.840
and also results in far more unique cards in&nbsp;
each deck. Multiply this by three to account&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:57.160
for the multiplayer aspect of the format, and&nbsp;
there will be a lot of cards to keep track of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:01.600
just as a baseline. Traditionally, veterans&nbsp;
of a given format will better be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.200
keep up with different things opponents&nbsp;
are playing due to wider card knowledge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.720
but with Wizards of the Coast increasing the&nbsp;
number of brand new cards they print into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:13.760
commander format from the high hundreds&nbsp;
per year to multiple thousand per year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:18.960
keeping up even as an experienced EDH player&nbsp;
can be difficult. My friend Hans built a funky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.680
new deck recently, and even as somebody who&nbsp;
basically thinks about EDH as a profession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:27.160
there were a surprising number of cards&nbsp;
from this deck that I had no familiarity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:32.480
with whatsoever. In a game with several decks&nbsp;
containing unfamiliar cards, just processing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.480
these new pieces of cardboard and figuring out&nbsp;
how to play against them in real time can eat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.440
up precious mental energy that could otherwise&nbsp;
go toward planning future turns, politicking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:47.480
and performing threat analysis on other cards.&nbsp;
And that’s with a group of experienced players.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:51.640
The new player experience in EDH is a whole&nbsp;
different beast. While the number of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:57.000
nonland cards in opponents’ decks in a 60 card&nbsp;
1v1 format might be somewhere in the 15-20 range,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:02.280
in EDH that number might be ten times that amount.&nbsp;
Getting a foothold in that environment will be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:06.120
difficult and time-consuming process even in&nbsp;
a setting with somewhat consistent decks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:11.200
players, and in an environment with rapidly&nbsp;
changing decks and players this is amplified.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:14.320
I’m not saying it’s impossible—there are&nbsp;
plenty of magic players who have learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:18.600
the game through EDH, and I’m sure there are&nbsp;
some who even thrived in this high stimulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:23.120
information-dense environment. There are also&nbsp;
likely plenty of people who learned the format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:26.840
somewhat more gradually, but who had plenty of fun&nbsp;
throughout the whole process even if they weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:32.000
keeping up 100% the whole time. I certainly don’t&nbsp;
think it’s worth anyone’s time and energy to push&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.000
players away from the commander format; it&nbsp;
has very clearly taken hold of casual Magic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.360
and there are lots of aspects of it that are&nbsp;
likely fun for newer players. What I am saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:44.560
though, is that increasing the card diversity&nbsp;
of decks does meaningfully increase the length&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:48.440
of time it’ll take for a new player to get&nbsp;
a foothold within the format. When I made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:53.080
my video about cEDH a few months back, I got&nbsp;
a number of interesting comments from people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:58.040
who prefer competitive EDH specifically because&nbsp;
there are fewer viable cards in the format that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:03.040
require keeping track of. A few of these were from&nbsp;
people who had jumped straight into cEDH from a 60&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:08.040
card format or a different card game entirely, and&nbsp;
this makes sense on some level—if you’re looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:11.480
to play commander seriously and don’t want&nbsp;
to have to bother with learning the thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:17.400
upon thousands of cards that see play in casual&nbsp;
EDH, cEDH is a nice alternative. It may even be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:21.880
as a casual EDH player in the comments pondered,&nbsp;
a smoother experience for those wanting to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:26.440
commander with strangers, for more reasons&nbsp;
than one. I see this as the most reasonable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:33.200
response to my main gripe with cEDH—“yes, fewer&nbsp;
cards are viable, and that’s good, actually.”

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:37.560
The issue of the EDH format’s card diversity is&nbsp;
compounded when decks are equally diverse in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:41.440
play patterns. The origins of the format were&nbsp;
people throwing together their bulk cards they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.560
couldn’t play in standard and playing them under&nbsp;
the banner of a commander that was vaguely-related&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:50.960
at best, and this likely resulted in unfocused&nbsp;
blobs of midrange being a dominant deck type,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:56.600
maybe with some funky synergies coming to the fore&nbsp;
here and there. And, as a casual pickup format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:00.960
this makes sense—if decks are built more to&nbsp;
produce relaxed games and showcase unusual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:05.760
cards than to win, the presence of those cards&nbsp;
can take center stage. Like sure, you might have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:10.720
to refresh yourself on what Freyalise Supplicant&nbsp;
does, but it’s probably not going to rapidly spell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:15.560
your downfall. However, as decks have gotten more&nbsp;
streamlined and have started trying to accomplish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:20.680
increasingly specific tasks increasingly quickly,&nbsp;
lacking knowledge of the ins and outs of what your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:26.120
opponent’s deck is doing starts to become more of&nbsp;
a problem. As you might expect, this effect also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:30.680
scales with power level up to a certain point.&nbsp;
If you’re playing against unaltered precons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:35.320
you can usually at least assume there will be some&nbsp;
sort of synergy-based midrange thing going on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:39.360
along with a couple predictable side synergy&nbsp;
packages. If you’re playing against a handful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:44.080
of mid-power midrange decks, there will likely be&nbsp;
less certainty and more surprises, but you still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:48.920
can probably get at least a rough idea of what’s&nbsp;
going on without knowing the opposing decks.

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:52.880
However, it’s the power level I’d describe as high&nbsp;
power casual where things start to feel really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:57.520
weird when playing with strangers. Infinite&nbsp;
combos, locks, and back-breakingly-powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:01.880
value engines get assembled on a whim and with&nbsp;
only relatively subtle hints to indicate their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:07.400
approach. Whereas in cEDH there is a smaller group&nbsp;
of viable strategies, with off-meta tech being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:12.800
spicy exception rather than the rule, high power&nbsp;
casual is packed to the rafters with off-meta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:18.120
because there isn’t a meaningful meta beyond some&nbsp;
archetypes being a bit more popular than others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:22.800
Now, I do love me some high power casual,&nbsp;
and I can even see a sort of weird allure in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:28.200
playing it with unfamiliar people and unfamiliar&nbsp;
decks—it’s a zany, explosive form of Magic, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:31.880
games are often quick and where there’s a ton of&nbsp;
sleuthing the ins and outs of opposing decks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:37.040
happens on the fly. But I’d also wager that this&nbsp;
is where a big dose of frustration comes from in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:42.040
pickup magic, particularly in games that straddle&nbsp;
the line between mid and high power, and where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:47.360
players enter with differing expectations. If&nbsp;
everybody is already needing to process the cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:52.080
and decks of three different opponents, and they&nbsp;
don’t know the cohesive plan of any one opponent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:56.320
let alone three… well, this is an environment&nbsp;
where it’s very easy to slip through a power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:00.680
play that opponents just aren’t equipped to&nbsp;
challenge. Sure, one could argue “just hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:06.160
up removal,” but that misunderstands the nature of&nbsp;
threat assessment. If I know my opponents’ decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:10.760
I can think to myself “okay, I’m 90% sure player&nbsp;
A doesn’t have the pieces to combo off on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:16.080
next turn, and player B should be two mana short&nbsp;
of a game-winning spell.” And then decisions can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:20.080
be made on whether to hold up interaction based&nbsp;
on if I have other useful plays to make that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:26.160
outweigh the risk. But, if instead of being 85%&nbsp;
sure it’s safe to tap out I have no firm estimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:32.760
whatsoever, what exactly am I supposed to do? Hold&nbsp;
up mana every turn? The answer is probably hold up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:37.760
mana when it’s nice to do so and don’t when it’s&nbsp;
not and maybe do my best to follow context clues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:41.800
but then on some level that’s just a random luck&nbsp;
check for whether or not I lose to some bullshit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:47.520
right? This effect is unfortunately amplified the&nbsp;
more unique ways of gaining advantage and winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:52.800
exist within a game—you can keep track of some&nbsp;
number of cards commonly associated with combos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:56.800
but when decks have plans that are weird or&nbsp;
unconventional there will be plenty of things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:02.120
will slip through the cracks. In competitive terms&nbsp;
you might call it brewer’s advantage, but in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:07.120
casual context it can create dynamics that aren’t&nbsp;
necessarily positive for everybody involved.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:10.840
With all that in mind, it might make sense that&nbsp;
the experience that gave me my love for weird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:16.320
offbeat EDH decks was actually an environment that&nbsp;
had basically none of these problems. From fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:22.400
of 2019 to summer of 2021 my playgroup of five&nbsp;
people played about 200 games with a set of ten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:26.640
budget decks, with each deck seeing an average&nbsp;
of one major rebuild over the course of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:31.560
two years. Though several of these decks were&nbsp;
quirky and evocative, and though the card choices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:36.520
in them got pretty funky, the fact that we were&nbsp;
exclusively playing with these ten guild-divided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:41.240
decks meant that the plans for each deck and&nbsp;
the cards used within those plans became all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:46.720
but public knowledge. Multiple players basically&nbsp;
learned the game through this pool, and despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:51.080
the diversity within the pool this was an ideal&nbsp;
environment for that—you could learn the game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:55.680
pieces and what each deck was doing reasonably&nbsp;
easily, and even if you’d forgotten something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:00.160
other players at the table were likely vocalizing&nbsp;
relevant information. If you don’t think about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:05.320
too hard, you might assume that this trait is&nbsp;
shared by a lot of casual playgroups. And yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:09.600
the best arguments against decks with unusual&nbsp;
cards and unique gameplans do relate to pick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:14.320
up games with largely unfamiliar opponents, but&nbsp;
plenty of these arguments also apply to games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:19.960
within dedicated playgroups. For one, players&nbsp;
these days often have a lot of decks—while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:24.760
the idea of building a second commander deck&nbsp;
was silly to me in 2015, a quick poll in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:29.320
Patron channel of my discord server showed&nbsp;
that around 60% of respondents had at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:34.680
8 decks. While the average is probably lower&nbsp;
than this, I have to imagine a lot of dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:38.920
playgroups still feature a hefty number&nbsp;
of unique decks among the various players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:43.960
well above the 10 seen at my table a few&nbsp;
years back. In addition, not all playgroups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:48.680
meet frequently enough to fully keep track of the&nbsp;
ins and outs of each others decks—while my small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:53.200
playgroup of 20-year olds played several games&nbsp;
a week at the height of pandemic-fueled boredom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:57.840
plenty of Magic players are busy adults with&nbsp;
time-consuming jobs, time-consuming children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:03.200
an extremely dedicated all-bard D&amp;D campaign,&nbsp;
or some combination of these things. There also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:07.440
might be a somewhat rotating player group,&nbsp;
with fresh decks being a regular sighting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:11.920
Under these sorts of circumstances, much of the&nbsp;
information overload present in pick up EDH will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:17.760
likely also be present. And, if this is the case,&nbsp;
one could argue that players playing simpler decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:23.360
with more commonly seen cards may very well&nbsp;
lead to more fun for everybody at the table.

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:27.840
So, that’s my case for why running funky,&nbsp;
unique decks is not an unquestioned good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:31.120
and can in fact amplify one of the&nbsp;
biggest problems with the casual EDH&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:36.760
format—the overwhelming amount of information&nbsp;
it presents players with. And let me clear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:42.440
I find this argument extremely interesting,&nbsp;
almost paralyzingly interesting. And yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:46.920
the deckbuilding conclusions that it gestures&nbsp;
at run almost perfectly contradictory to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:51.760
hipster tendencies that all but define my&nbsp;
deck building style. So, how do we square&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:56.840
that circle? Well for starters, in all of the&nbsp;
examples I gave of what might constitute a bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.840 --> 00:14:01.280
experience as a result of decks being weird&nbsp;
and unusual, there are tons of other factors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:05.840
that might contribute to the information density&nbsp;
at the table. For example, I emphasize that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:11.600
issue is vastly more problematic in settings with&nbsp;
strangers and unknown decks, but the unknowability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:17.800
of decks isn’t a set in stone thing. You and your&nbsp;
opponents can tell each other what your decks do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:21.600
and you can go into whatever amount of detail&nbsp;
suits the decks present and suits the game you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:25.960
want to play. Whenever I play with new people&nbsp;
at my local game store I’ll generally give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:30.680
some explanation of the deck I’m playing while I’m&nbsp;
shuffling up, and I’ll often ask for the same from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:36.560
my opponents. Even when it’s trading relatively&nbsp;
simple explanations, this act can allow players to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:41.680
go into games with a sense of groundedness, having&nbsp;
at least some idea of what they’re getting into.

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:45.600
There are also additional actions during games&nbsp;
that can further elucidate what’s happening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:50.920
within a game, and a relatively basic one I’ve&nbsp;
seen pretty often is verbally differentiating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:56.360
commonly-game-ending spells from other spells.&nbsp;
For example, saying “I play a Llanowar Elves”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.360 --> 00:15:02.280
versus “I attempt to cast Scapeshift.” I like this&nbsp;
a lot because it’s not even an insult to the game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:06.720
knowledge of your opponents—on the contrary,&nbsp;
you’re treating them like seasoned veterans of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:11.880
playing against your deck who would know that&nbsp;
you resolving Scapeshift is a problem, or at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:16.680
least something worth paying full attention to.&nbsp;
I’ll even sometimes draw attention to important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:20.560
information I’ve revealed—when I’m playing&nbsp;
my Francisco Malcolm deck against people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:26.280
haven’t played against it much before, if I reveal&nbsp;
Shred Memory off of an explore on turn 4, I might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:30.840
say something like “I’ll keep Shred Memory on top,&nbsp;
there’s a particular two mana card I might want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:36.560
transmute for sometime soon,” as a gentle reminder&nbsp;
of what my deck is trying to do. Now, that’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:41.120
only deck I give that amount of information for&nbsp;
since it’s a deceptively fast combo deck that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:46.120
generally on the more powerful end of my store’s&nbsp;
high-power casual pods, but that’s just an example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:51.520
of how giving extra information can reduce&nbsp;
whiplash and lead to a better play experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:56.480
Another method I’ve commonly seen is keeping&nbsp;
revealed cards revealed longer than is required,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.480 --> 00:16:00.320
most often putting a revealed card into&nbsp;
hand face up for a turn or two or leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:05.360
the revealed top card of a library face-up until&nbsp;
it’s drawn. This likewise removes a bit of mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:10.600
strain from opponents by not requiring them to&nbsp;
immediately commit revealed cards to memory.

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:14.360
Some of this might seem a bit obvious, but I&nbsp;
feel the need to say it explicitly because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:18.320
got a lot of confusing comments on my&nbsp;
commander-as-a-combo-piece video from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:24.240
players who seem to think that deception is a&nbsp;
key feature of the EDH format. Not bluffing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:29.120
like purposefully concealing key details about&nbsp;
the cards contained within one’s deck for purposes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:33.800
of pulling off a “gotcha” win. This is&nbsp;
obviously fine in competitive formats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:38.520
since there is a meta/countermeta dynamic at play,&nbsp;
and I imagine some people enjoy playing this way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:44.640
in casual EDH as well. However, to reiterate an&nbsp;
earlier point, decks being unknown makes threat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:50.040
assessment vastly more difficult, and as a result&nbsp;
injects a lot of extra luck into games, and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:54.720
certainly don’t find that enjoyable personally.&nbsp;
I also find the idea of trying to sneak through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:59.080
an explosive win against a table who’s not&nbsp;
prepared for or looking for that kind of EDH&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.080 --> 00:17:05.000
to be a little distasteful, but that’s subjective&nbsp;
territory and I can’t claim to know the desires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:10.080
of any specific EDH table, so I won’t throw&nbsp;
any extra words at that particular subject.

00:17:10.080 --> 00:17:13.600
Beyond the gameplay methods for getting around&nbsp;
this information overload, there can also be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:17.600
attributes of the decks at the table that&nbsp;
affect the degree of information present.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:23.000
Is this the part where I find yet another excuse&nbsp;
to tell you to play more removal? Well, kind of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:28.200
but not exactly. The other big thing that made&nbsp;
the $25 deck pool I used to play with so good for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:33.120
avoiding information overload was that the decks&nbsp;
were interaction dense, as well as low-slung and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:37.720
grindy to be able to handle the aforementioned&nbsp;
interaction. The fact that the pool began with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:42.600
two boardwipe-dense control decks meant that&nbsp;
every midrange deck was designed with the goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:47.240
of not overcommitting to the board, which means&nbsp;
fewer nonland permanents on board and less of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:52.440
the escalation-type cards that typically pump&nbsp;
mid-sized midrange boards up to being alarming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:57.680
masses of assorted permanents in a lot of casual&nbsp;
EDH games. I should be clear—this worked well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:02.800
because these 10 decks formed a little meta,&nbsp;
and your mileage may vary in multiple regards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:08.200
if you try to emulate any of this in a pod of&nbsp;
more conventionally-built EDH decks. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.200 --> 00:18:12.240
it does gesture at a vision for EDH that’s&nbsp;
much more approachable than a lot of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:17.360
I see at local game stores, and while it’s not&nbsp;
necessarily a vision that can or should be copied,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:21.880
it gives a useful benchmark to reference&nbsp;
for what EDH can look like. When removal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.880 --> 00:18:26.680
is absent and everybody is just allowed to&nbsp;
do their deck’s snowbally midrange thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:31.360
boardstates often end up utterly caked in the&nbsp;
extra noise of various synergy permanents and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:35.880
utility creatures and tokens, and I think&nbsp;
it’s always worth asking the question of if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.880 --> 00:18:40.520
that’s a worthwhile way to play, and if there&nbsp;
are ways to avoid that sort of boardstate.

00:18:40.520 --> 00:18:46.040
Beyond my practical recommendations, I also&nbsp;
think it’s worth considering what EDH represents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.040 --> 00:18:50.640
Because you may have noticed that the potential&nbsp;
version of EDH I gestured at earlier in the video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:55.920
one where decks operate within a narrower set&nbsp;
of potential gameplans with a narrower range of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.920 --> 00:19:02.000
cards, is one that more closely resembles 60 card&nbsp;
formats. Chasing this vision for the format might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:07.400
have its benefits in some ways, but at what cost?&nbsp;
There are so many things that can only be done in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:14.160
EDH, and so many cards that aren’t really viable,&nbsp;
or even legal, in most other formats. I think it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.160 --> 00:19:18.800
worth considering the case that EDH can be an&nbsp;
overwhelming format at times, and I think it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:24.160
also worth considering how to mitigate those&nbsp;
problems. It might even be worthwhile for newer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:28.840
players to try and sprinkle some variety into&nbsp;
their MTG diet—I’m of the opinion that almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:32.880
all Magic players can benefit from playing&nbsp;
limited, and cube can give a nice way to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:36.720
limited without feeling like you’re wasting your&nbsp;
money on packs that you haven’t yet developed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:42.720
skill to fully utilize. But no, reducing the&nbsp;
variety of the format isn’t the solution, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:48.040
it isn’t worth it. The creation of the original&nbsp;
Elder Dragon Highlander format is often credited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:53.760
to Adam Staley, and Staley conceptualized the&nbsp;
format as a way to inject variety and creativity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:58.760
into a game whose constructive formats were&nbsp;
already rigid and meta-driven even in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:03.600
few years of the game. Singleton meant&nbsp;
being forced to play underutilized cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:08.200
a wide card pool meant decks could be constructed&nbsp;
with basically any old cards sitting around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:12.680
and a higher life total meant more space for&nbsp;
creative but slower strategies that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:17.800
likely get squeezed out by faster decks in a 20&nbsp;
health format. An Elder Dragon at the head of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:24.000
things gave an undeniable symbolic core to decks.&nbsp;
A format as large as EDH can’t be solely governed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:36.280
by the vision of one man 30 years ago, but you&nbsp;
have to admit—he was kind of onto something.

